11. Your Bug Bounty Checklist from This Chapter
When testing any API, do this:

Check for injection in every parameter (query, body, headers, cookies):

Single quote '

Double quote "

Backtick `

Semicolon ;

Double dash --

' OR 1=1 --

1; WAITFOR DELAY '00:00:05' --

Check error messages:

Do they reveal DB type, file paths, library versions?

Are they different for “user not found” vs “wrong password”?

Check security headers:

Use curl -I https://api.example.com/endpoint

Look for missing X-Content-Type-Options, CSP, X-Frame-Options

Check for reflected input:

Send <script>alert(1)</script> in any parameter.

See if it appears in response (even in JSON).

Check Content-Type handling:

Send Content-Type: text/plain with JSON body – does it still work? (possible SSRF or desync)

Check for mass assignment (not in this chapter but relevant):

Add extra fields to JSON – does it update unexpected DB columns?

12. Key Quotes to Remember
“Prepared statements eliminate SQL injection if used consistently.”

“Be strict in what you accept – require correct Content-Type, validate inputs.”

“Never echo user input back without validation – XSS hides in JSON responses.”

“Error responses are often the weakest link – fix headers and content there too.”

